SUMMARY="Go compiler, standard library and tools for Haiku ARM64"
DESCRIPTION="Native Go 1.27.4 toolchain for Haiku ARM64 (go, gofmt, the standard \
library source, and the compile/link/asm/cgo/vet/cover/fix tool binaries), cross-built \
with scripts/cross-arm64-golang.sh against a from-scratch RenkuOS/Go haiku/arm64 \
runtime port -- this image had no prior Go support of any kind, and HaikuPorts has no \
arm64 repository to depend on either. GOROOT is self-contained under lib/go; bin/gofmt \
is a plain symlink into it, the same layout rust_bin uses for cargo/rustc. Revision 4 \
made bin/go a wrapper instead (the real binary moved to lib/go/bin/go-real), fixing two \
things found building a real third-party web app: (1) this toolchain shipped with both \
GOPROXY and GOSUMDB blank, so every module fetch failed outright (\"GOPROXY list is not \
the empty string, but contains no entries\" / \"missing GOSUMDB\") until a user set them \
by hand -- the wrapper now defaults both, same as a real Go install would, without \
overriding a caller that already set them. (2) github.com/gin-gonic/gin (the most-used Go \
web framework) failed to build at all: its default logger calls into \
github.com/mattn/go-isatty, which ships one real/stub IsTerminal implementation per OS \
family and has none that matches haiku, so IsTerminal/IsCygwinTerminal were undefined at \
compile time -- confirmed building a real gin.Default() app with the vanilla module. \
Fixed by adding haiku to the existing \"unsupported platform, just return false\" stub \
(isatty_others.go, same safe default already used for js/wasm/appengine/tinygo/nacl -- \
correct here too, since a terminal-color check over an SSH-piped session should say \"not \
a terminal\" anyway) and shipping the patched module, repackaged at the same path and \
version, as a local GOPROXY-protocol override directory the wrapper puts ahead of the \
real proxy in GOPROXY. Revision 5 adds the same treatment for golang.org/x/sys, which \
has no haiku support in any published version at all, breaking any module that imports \
golang.org/x/sys/unix directly (a far larger share of the ecosystem than go-isatty's own \
gap) -- ported from korli/go's own sibling fork korli/sys_haiku (its master-haiku branch, \
amd64 only) plus this port's own already-existing arm64 extension of the same files \
(cmd/vendor/golang.org/x/sys/unix/*_arm64* in this port's own src/ tree), applied onto a \
clean checkout of each of v0.41.0 through v0.50.0 (ten versions, covering roughly the \
last year and a half of releases, since the override is keyed by exact version and a \
dependency graph can resolve to any of them via Go's minimum-version-selection -- \
confirmed this matters: the same direct-unix.Stat program that builds clean against the \
patched v0.50.0 fails with undefined: unix.Stat_t against unpatched v0.20.0, fetched from \
the real proxy since that version isn't in the override). GONOSUMDB covers both \
overridden modules, since their content deliberately differs from the public checksum \
database. See scripts/golang-goproxy-overrides/README.md for both patches and how to \
extend this to another module with the same kind of gap. Confirmed end to end from a \
clean module cache: fresh go mod init, go get github.com/gin-gonic/gin, go build, run, \
and a real HTTP 200 from curl against a gin.Default() handler; separately, a small \
program built against the patched x/sys/unix tree and run on the real target machine \
over SSH, exercising Stat, a full Socket/Bind/Getsockname round trip, and \
IoctlGetTermios -- no manual replace directive, no pre-seeded cache, just this package's \
own defaults. Also confirmed, on the same real, already-installed package over plain SSH \
(no desktop session): a clean-cache \`go build std\` (the entire standard library, many \
real fork/execs) using the package's own native bin/haiku_arm64/go to build itself, exit \
0. Revision 9 fixes CGO_ENABLED=1 entirely: linking any cgo program failed with \
\`relocation R_AARCH64_ADR_PREL_PG_HI21 against symbol 'poll' which may bind externally \
... recompile with -fPIC\`, traced to cmd/link/internal/ld/data.go's dynreloc(): Go \
forces FlagD (suppress dynamic-loader-format output) whenever linking externally, which \
cgo always does, and that unconditionally skipped the dynrelocsym() pass that rewrites a \
cgo_import_dynamic data symbol's address-of relocation (e.g. runtime's own libc_poll, \
netpoll_haiku.go) to go through the GOT -- needed on Haiku's libc-call-style port \
specifically, since the compiler emits a plain page-relative ADRP+ADD for this and relies \
on that linker-side rewrite to happen before handing the object to gcc/ld. Fixed by \
running just that one Textp relocation-rewrite loop for Haiku even when FlagD is set, \
while still skipping everything else FlagD normally skips (building a real \
.dynsym/.hash/.got.plt too, tried first, conflicts with producing a plain \
external-linkable object and crashed the linker's own object writer with \`internal \
error: ElfShdr relocSect\`). Confirmed end to end: a cgo program calling a real C \
function and converting a C string back to Go (C.add, C.CString/C.GoString) builds and \
runs correctly, and the existing goroutine/os-exec/go-isatty smoke tests plus a full \
\`go build std\` still pass with no regression. Revision 10 makes signals work at all: \
before it, a Go program ignored SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP (only kill -9 stopped it) and \
os/signal.Notify never fired, because every signal Go handled left the receiving thread \
dead in sigtramp. runtime/sys_haiku_arm64.s's sigtramp had copied the amd64 frame layout \
(arguments at 0/8/16(SP)): on arm64 0(RSP) is the function's own saved return address, \
so storing the signal number there meant sigtramp could never return, and the plain BL \
to sigtrampgo went through its ABI0 wrapper, which reads its arguments from \
8/16/24(RSP), so sigtrampgo saw the siginfo pointer as the signal number. Fixed by moving \
the frame up by 8 bytes and calling sigtrampgo/badsignal through their register-ABI \
entry points, as the linux/darwin arm64 ports do. Two more bugs then showed up: \
ucontext_t's uc_mcontext is at offset 48 (struct vregs holds __uint128_t, so it is \
16-byte aligned), not 40, so every register read from a signal context was one slot off; \
and pthread_self() went through libcCall, whose int32 result truncated the pthread_t \
pointer, so the SIGURG of async preemption made pthread_kill fault. g is now also kept in \
TLS slot 62 (as on haiku/amd64) and loaded from there in sigtramp, instead of trusting \
R28 inside libc code. With all of that fixed, the two earlier workarounds (no Go \
SIGCHLD handler, async preemption off) are gone: SIGCHLD and SIGURG land in libc calls \
safely, os/exec works with both on, and a goroutine spinning in a loop with no calls is \
preempted again. The startup debug output (\"234567\" on stderr at every start) is gone \
too: runtime/asm_arm64.s is upstream's again. The same revision also \
carries the cgo TLS fix: with cgo, runtime.save_g/load_g kept g at TPIDR_EL0 + 16, \
which on Haiku is TLS slot 2, errno, so C code that set errno and then called back \
into Go loaded a garbage g and hung; runtime/cgo (gcc_haiku_arm64.c) now gives g a \
slot from tls_allocate() instead (non-cgo programs keep slot 62). And the pure Go \
resolver now reads Haiku's hosts file, /boot/system/settings/network/hosts (there is \
no /etc/hosts), so \"localhost\" resolves without DNS."
HOMEPAGE="https://go.dev/"
VERSION="1.27.4"
REVISION="10"
LICENSE="BSD (3-clause)"
COPYRIGHT="2009 The Go Authors"
ARCHITECTURES_SUPPORTED="arm64"

PROVIDES="golang_bin = $VERSION
cmd:go = $VERSION
cmd:gofmt = $VERSION"
REQUIRES="haiku"

# Provenance of the upstream source this was built from -- not a
# haikuporter SOURCE_URI/CHECKSUM_SHA256 this recipe can actually be
# built with (see NOTE below), but the exact same values HaikuPorts'
# own dev-lang/golang recipe uses for the x86_64 build, confirmed by
# downloading and hashing this URL directly: the arm64 runtime port in
# this port's own src/ tree is applied on top of exactly this tree, not
# a different one.
SOURCE_URI="https://github.com/korli/go/archive/go1.27.4-haiku.tar.gz"
SOURCE_DIR="go-go1.27.4-haiku"
CHECKSUM_SHA256="f3d5c413020820a685800a5d665212661db14889e852ace571599ab28cc8fde1"

NOTE="cross-built, not haikuporter-built: see scripts/cross-arm64-golang.sh for how \
the .hpkg under prebuilt/arm64/ was produced. SOURCE_URI/CHECKSUM_SHA256 above are \
provenance only (what korli/go source this port's own src/ files were applied onto), \
not something haikuporter can actually build from with a plain BUILD()/INSTALL(): the \
arm64 runtime port itself (the files under this repo's own src/, copied on top of that \
source tree file for file) and the host-vs-native dual-toolchain build \
(GOOS=haiku GOARCH=arm64 ./make.bash against a linux/arm64 bootstrap compiler, run inside \
the project's own haiku-builder Docker container) aren't expressible in that format."

PREBUILT_PACKAGES="pkgman-repo/prebuilt/arm64/golang_bin-$VERSION-$REVISION-arm64.hpkg"
